WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.330
We will study the development of the gastrointestinal tract and the peritoneal cavity and peritoneum in the developmental movement of the stomach, duodenum, intestine, and colon.

2
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:18.090
We will begin by understanding it in the development of the stomach in relation to the liver, spleen, and pancreas.

3
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:29.000
Let's understand the initial phase, what we call the delimitation of the embryo. We saw the integration.

4
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.960
of this external coelom into an internal coelom. We arrive at this stage.

5
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.520
And here, we see both in the sagittal view and in the transverse view.

6
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.010
We clearly see the integration of my peritoneal cavity.

7
00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.730
Don't forget the synchronicity of development.

8
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.450
One last image to see this integration.

9
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:54.090
The main driver is growth, it's the formation of our cortex, but that's elsewhere.

10
00:00:56.142 --> 00:01:01.282
primitive, the overall growth of my embryo, the coiling and return to my midline.

11
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:10.792
When you take a small baby, you hold it in your hands and it shows you the whole history of its embryology. You just have to follow the tissues. It's

12
00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:21.282
a three-dimensional movement in four and even in the fifth dimension. I mean by fourth dimension, time. The fifth dimension, I mean that it is this energetic,

13
00:01:22.622 --> 00:01:26.862
electrical component. overall that underlies the entire system.

14
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:38.333
Don't forget that this movement is always integrated into this ascending phase of my ectoderm and relative descent of my endoderm, with this phase almost at the level of the heart.

15
00:01:40.513 --> 00:01:45.703
The ascent of the ectoderm and the descent of the endoderm and the integration of the heart

16
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:51.863
stretch the primordia of the diaphragmatic musculature and those of the future muscles.

17
00:01:52.284 --> 00:02:01.884
This means that to treat and reorganize this entire space, you will need to understand that you will have to treat and release the diaphragm, because it is the one that will create

18
00:02:02.524 --> 00:02:04.564
this global delimitation.

19
00:02:05.544 --> 00:02:12.024
The ascent of my neural tube, the development of my peritoneal cavity, the placement of my heart is

20
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:20.284
a movement that will reintegrate globally towards a point of attachment.

21
00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:29.595
which we call the umbilical cord. We could say that the umbilical cord, in its finality, contains the history of this development, of this unfolding, of

22
00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:35.835
this delimitation, of this reorganization of your peritoneum.

23
00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:45.315
This will integrate into specific rotational movements, organized in clockwise and counter-clockwise directions. On a frontal plane, we will see a

24
00:02:48.426 --> 00:02:58.826
global counter-clockwise developmental movement. On a transverse plane, we will see a clockwise developmental movement. This peritoneal cavity will begin to organize,

25
00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:11.426
to delimit itself. This means that it will move, and in this movement, you will have to learn to follow this development. First, there is the peritoneum that surrounds an organ. This

26
00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:13.756
is called a visceral peritoneum.

27
00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:22.866
When a peritoneum... connects an organ to the posterior part, it is called a mesentery. If it's the stomach, it will be called a mesogastrium.

28
00:03:24.857 --> 00:03:36.217
If it's the duodenum, it will be called the mesoduodenum. If it's a colon, it will be called the mesocolon. Or, depending on the location, it will be called a fascia of Tolte, for example.

29
00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:42.527
But if it's a small intestine, it's called a mesentery. Peritoneum that has the...

30
00:03:44.568 --> 00:03:49.628
Here, there is the posterior part, it is called the posterior parietal peritoneum.

31
00:03:50.988 --> 00:03:56.828
Laterally, it is called the lateral parietal peritoneum, the anterior parietal peritoneum.

32
00:03:59.668 --> 00:04:05.188
If it is a peritoneum that connects one organ to another organ, it is called an omentum.

33
00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:12.788
For example, you have the lesser omentum, which is a tissue that connects the liver to the stomach.

34
00:04:12.788 --> 00:04:16.338
You also have an omentum that connects the spleen to the stomach.

35
00:04:16.338 --> 00:04:22.018
An omentum, a mesentery, is always a double layer that carries a vessel right in the middle.

36
00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:34.269
But in the developmental movement, we will see that there will be folds and a reorganization that makes the omentum that is here, for example the mesentery, able to come to the side like this.

37
00:04:42.911 --> 00:04:47.591
And so here, there is a tissue that will attach and it will form an adhesion fascia.

38
00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:54.011
This is something that surgeons can resect without problem because there are no vessels.

39
00:04:54.011 --> 00:04:56.851
A mesentery always contains a vessel.

40
00:04:56.851 --> 00:05:01.211
Whereas a ligament or a fascia does not necessarily contain vessels.

41
00:05:01.211 --> 00:05:09.531
At the level of the peritoneum, we will have the mesoderm, the somatopleure, the splanchnopleure, the mesogastrium, ventral d'or...

42
00:05:09.531 --> 00:05:15.561
and we will have what are called mesenteries, fascias, omenta that we call epiploons or ligaments.

43
00:05:17.782 --> 00:05:23.832
All of that is the hyperperitoneum, we could say, initially organized in the peritoneal cavity, I mean.

44
00:05:24.102 --> 00:05:31.382
My intestine will start to grow, my liver will develop, my stomach wants to express itself, my lungs are growing.

45
00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:34.962
All of this needs to find its place.

